1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:02,090
Every generation before

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:03,450
yours had to die.

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,870
Yours may be the first that won't.

4
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:07,660
Because labs now treat death

5
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,340
as an engineering problem.

6
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,280
And they've pointed AI at it.

7
00:00:11,770 --> 00:00:13,540
So picture the day it works.

8
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:15,170
The offer is in front of you.

9
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:16,450
No more aging.

10
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,570
No end date.

11
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:19,050
Yes, or no.

12
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:21,050
If you could stop dying,

13
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:22,160
would you?

14
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:23,680
Most people say no.

15
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:24,720
But the labs aren't

16
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:26,120
waiting for an answer.

17
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:27,250
Some want to copy

18
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:28,910
a mind into a computer.

19
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:30,100
And they've started.

20
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:31,330
With a fly.

21
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,030
A real fly's brain was mapped,

22
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:35,590
wire by wire.

23
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:36,640
This year,

24
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:38,000
a simple computer model

25
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:39,260
built from that map

26
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,650
began to steer a virtual body.

27
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:42,850
There's a second way

28
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:44,000
to stop dying.

29
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:45,300
Closer.

30
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:47,660
Repair the body you already have.

31
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,350
And it comes with a date.

32
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:50,940
2032.

33
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:53,460
The date comes from Ray Kurzweil,

34
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:54,480
the inventor

35
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,360
and longtime futurist.

36
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:06,600
His claim: a year back

37
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,290
for every year you live.

38
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,100
That's what "won't die"

39
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:11,140
means here.

40
00:01:11,250 --> 00:01:12,500
Not immortal.

41
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,570
An accident could still kill you.

42
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:16,100
Old age couldn't.

43
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:17,680
But Kurzweil has moved

44
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:18,920
his date before,

45
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:19,850
and he sells books

46
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:21,000
about this future.

47
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:23,370
Critics note that no peer-reviewed

48
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:25,230
study stands behind it.

49
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:27,260
And at today's pace of progress,

50
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:28,470
one blogger's analysis

51
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:29,650
from this summer lands

52
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:31,350
somewhere else entirely.

53
00:01:31,510 --> 00:01:32,490
A leading aging

54
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:33,890
researcher shared it.

55
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,740
Its result: not 2032.

56
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,020
Around 2459.

57
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:41,050
In your lifetime.

58
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,060
Or four centuries too late.

59
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:46,410
And even that analysis says:

60
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:48,000
no chance, unless

61
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:49,710
AI speeds things up.

62
00:01:49,970 --> 00:01:51,380
So the first generation

63
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:54,150
that won't die is a bet on AI.

64
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,000
Is that bet paying off?

65
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,010
There are early signs.

66
00:01:58,210 --> 00:01:59,460
AI models are learning

67
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,150
to predict how a living cell

68
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:02,480
reacts to a drug,

69
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:04,810
before anyone runs the experiment.

70
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:05,770
And in a study

71
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:07,270
published this October,

72
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:08,450
machine learning helped

73
00:02:08,490 --> 00:02:09,530
researchers pick out

74
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:10,910
a single gene.

75
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:13,110
They boosted it in old mice.

76
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:16,260
The mice lived about 14% longer.

77
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:18,060
A small study.

78
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:19,900
Female mice only.

79
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:21,510
Not yet repeated.

80
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:23,500
And no drug against aging

81
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:25,680
itself is approved anywhere.

82
00:02:25,810 --> 00:02:27,730
So the date is a guess.

83
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:29,190
The work is real.

84
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:30,820
But what if the repairs

85
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:32,250
come too late for you?

86
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:35,990
Then the copy is what's left.

87
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:37,380
Back to that fly.

88
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:38,350
This March,

89
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:40,050
a San Francisco startup plugged

90
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:41,480
that simple brain model

91
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,130
into a virtual body.

92
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:45,350
And the model began to choose.

93
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:46,300
Walk.

94
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,130
Groom.

95
00:02:47,170 --> 00:02:48,040
Feed.

96
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,310
The startup's headline:

97
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:51,340
we've uploaded a fruit fly.

98
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:53,090
Critics said no.

99
00:02:53,250 --> 00:02:54,600
Even a foundation that works

100
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:56,060
toward brain uploads.

101
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:58,100
Their point: the model has none

102
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,730
of the real fly's memories,

103
00:02:59,770 --> 00:03:00,930
and separate software

104
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:01,970
does the walking.

105
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:03,900
The company's own chief executive

106
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:06,160
is frank about how little went in.

107
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:15,090
And still.

108
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:18,030
A map of a brain, switched on,

109
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:20,280
choosing what a body does next.

110
00:03:21,330 --> 00:03:22,300
The same executive

111
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:24,210
says that, optimistically,

112
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:25,590
the first human uploads

113
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:28,160
are a few years away, not decades.

114
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:30,270
He also has a startup to fund.

115
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:32,320
Yet of a human brain,

116
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:33,270
science has mapped

117
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:35,220
one cubic millimeter.

118
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:36,470
A crumb.

119
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:37,860
Its data filled more

120
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,550
than a million gigabytes.

121
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,640
But suppose it worked.

122
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:42,960
A copy wakes up

123
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:44,450
with all your memories.

124
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:45,760
Is that you?

125
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:46,950
Or did you die,

126
00:03:46,990 --> 00:03:47,910
while something else

127
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:49,440
remembers being you?

128
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:51,440
So ask it again.

129
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,710
If the only way to stop dying

130
00:03:53,750 --> 00:03:56,180
were to become a copy, would you?

131
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:58,230
Which brings back

132
00:03:58,270 --> 00:04:00,170
the answer most people give.

133
00:04:00,610 --> 00:04:01,540
No.

134
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:04,290
In 2013, a major American survey

135
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:05,460
asked about treatments

136
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,170
to live to 120.

137
00:04:07,510 --> 00:04:09,390
Most said, "not for me."

138
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:10,680
Yet most believed

139
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,700
that other people would say yes.

140
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:15,040
It seems everybody wants this.

141
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:17,230
Except everybody you ask.

142
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:19,160
What holds the yes back?

143
00:04:19,470 --> 00:04:21,080
This August, a new survey

144
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:22,690
turned the question around.

145
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:24,800
Would you accept a shorter life,

146
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:26,520
if it came without decline?

147
00:04:26,790 --> 00:04:27,980
No failing body.

148
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:29,370
No fading mind.

149
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:32,160
Almost 2 in 3 said yes.

150
00:04:32,730 --> 00:04:35,250
So the fear isn't living forever.

151
00:04:35,390 --> 00:04:37,410
It's being old forever.

152
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:39,650
This channel exists

153
00:04:39,690 --> 00:04:41,020
for one reason:

154
00:04:41,210 --> 00:04:42,070
so that the arrival

155
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:43,190
of superintelligence

156
00:04:43,230 --> 00:04:45,030
goes well for everybody.

157
00:04:45,210 --> 00:04:46,610
Healthy years included.

158
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:47,840
Want that too?

159
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:48,920
Then subscribe.

160
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,340
So ask it the fair way.

161
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,240
If you could stop dying,

162
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:55,250
and stay healthy, would you?

163
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:56,690
Even then, there's

164
00:04:56,730 --> 00:04:58,500
a serious case for no.

165
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,050
One philosopher argued

166
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:01,400
that an endless life

167
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:02,660
ends in boredom.

168
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:04,160
Others answer that some

169
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:05,830
pleasures never wear out.

170
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:08,790
But suppose enough people say yes.

171
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:10,210
What happens to a world

172
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:12,110
that was built around dying?

173
00:05:13,270 --> 00:05:14,450
Say it works.

174
00:05:14,690 --> 00:05:15,740
In this scenario,

175
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:17,570
you're older than anyone alive

176
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:19,180
today, and healthy.

177
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:21,280
Take a promise you may have made:

178
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,160
till death do us part.

179
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:24,370
For most of history,

180
00:05:24,410 --> 00:05:25,290
that usually meant

181
00:05:25,330 --> 00:05:26,960
less than 30 years.

182
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:29,110
Now it has no end date.

183
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:29,820
More time

184
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:31,210
with the person you love.

185
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,140
And a much bigger promise.

186
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:34,270
And a life that long

187
00:05:34,310 --> 00:05:35,690
has to be paid for.

188
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:36,770
A London professor

189
00:05:36,810 --> 00:05:38,470
who studies long lives says

190
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:41,490
the old plan, learn, work, retire,

191
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:42,540
is breaking.

192
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,620
So you could have five careers.

193
00:05:55,670 --> 00:05:57,480
And you might never be done.

194
00:05:57,650 --> 00:05:58,750
And what your parents

195
00:05:58,790 --> 00:05:59,790
would have left you?

196
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:02,390
In this scenario, it never comes.

197
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:03,710
Your parents stay.

198
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:04,990
That's the gain.

199
00:06:05,170 --> 00:06:07,080
What they own stays with them.

200
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:08,290
That's the price.

201
00:06:08,450 --> 00:06:09,450
Still yes?

202
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:11,660
And an inheritance

203
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:13,640
is only the small version.

204
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:15,830
Power and ideas change hands

205
00:06:15,870 --> 00:06:16,870
the same way:

206
00:06:17,130 --> 00:06:18,470
when someone goes.

207
00:06:18,810 --> 00:06:20,550
Max Planck, the physicist,

208
00:06:20,590 --> 00:06:21,940
saw it in science.

209
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:23,800
A new truth, he wrote,

210
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:24,550
doesn't win by

211
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:26,300
convincing its opponents.

212
00:06:26,430 --> 00:06:27,400
It wins because

213
00:06:27,440 --> 00:06:28,900
they eventually die.

214
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:31,290
Economists later tested that.

215
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:33,620
When a star scientist died early,

216
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:34,890
outsiders moved in,

217
00:06:34,930 --> 00:06:36,990
and did unusually strong work.

218
00:06:37,260 --> 00:06:38,780
That doesn't make death good.

219
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:40,420
It shows that people at the top

220
00:06:40,460 --> 00:06:42,190
can keep newcomers out,

221
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:43,450
just by staying.

222
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,550
And at the very top,

223
00:06:45,590 --> 00:06:47,790
this subject has already come up.

224
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:50,990
Beijing, September 2025.

225
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:52,640
A military parade,

226
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:54,080
a live microphone.

227
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:55,390
China's president

228
00:06:55,430 --> 00:06:56,520
and Russia's president

229
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:58,580
walk toward the viewing platform.

230
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:00,280
Through his interpreter,

231
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:02,360
Russia's president talks of organs

232
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:04,590
transplanted again and again,

233
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:05,580
of people growing

234
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:07,580
younger, even immortal.

235
00:07:07,840 --> 00:07:09,330
China's president answers

236
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:10,280
that some predict

237
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:11,280
humans may live

238
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:13,570
to 150 this century.

239
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:16,280
This isn't about two countries.

240
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:17,610
Any president.

241
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:18,700
Any judge.

242
00:07:18,760 --> 00:07:19,800
Any founder.

243
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:21,090
Who steps aside,

244
00:07:21,130 --> 00:07:22,660
if they never have to?

245
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:24,410
And who gets it at all?

246
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:26,850
In that survey from 2013,

247
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:28,560
a clear majority expected

248
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:30,340
that only the wealthy would.

249
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:31,750
Now ask yourself.

250
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:33,600
Would you still say yes,

251
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:35,780
if the powerful said yes first?

252
00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:37,280
If you could afford it,

253
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:38,620
and your neighbor couldn't?

254
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,580
It doesn't have to go that way.

255
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:42,840
Start with what people

256
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:44,450
actually said yes to.

257
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:45,620
Not forever.

258
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:46,820
Healthy.

259
00:07:46,860 --> 00:07:47,770
Around the world,

260
00:07:47,810 --> 00:07:49,610
people spend close to 10 years

261
00:07:49,650 --> 00:07:51,280
of their lives in poor health.

262
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,210
Win those years back first,

263
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:53,810
and you shrink

264
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:55,550
the very thing people fear:

265
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,150
the long decline.

266
00:07:57,720 --> 00:07:59,570
But healthy years only count

267
00:07:59,610 --> 00:08:01,290
if people can afford them.

268
00:08:01,450 --> 00:08:02,720
Some medical technology

269
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:04,300
gets cheap fast.

270
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,270
Reading a human genome once

271
00:08:06,310 --> 00:08:09,110
cost about $95 million.

272
00:08:09,310 --> 00:08:10,950
Today, a few hundred.

273
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:13,290
Yet so far, in America,

274
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:14,600
new medical technology

275
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:15,950
has pushed health costs

276
00:08:15,990 --> 00:08:17,400
up, not down.

277
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:19,960
Which pattern wins is still open.

278
00:08:20,230 --> 00:08:22,380
Part of that comes down to rules.

279
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:24,200
Labs don't write those.

280
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:26,210
Voters and lawmakers do.

281
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:28,050
A few exist already.

282
00:08:28,630 --> 00:08:30,050
Who steps aside?

283
00:08:30,270 --> 00:08:32,080
American presidents must,

284
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:33,500
after two terms.

285
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:35,680
Who pays for longer lives?

286
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:36,940
Denmark has tied

287
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:38,860
its retirement age by law

288
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:40,610
to how long people live.

289
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:42,670
And who can afford it?

290
00:08:42,850 --> 00:08:44,400
A Montana law now lets

291
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:45,690
licensed clinics sell

292
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:47,090
treatments after just

293
00:08:47,130 --> 00:08:48,840
a first safety trial.

294
00:08:49,110 --> 00:08:50,680
In return, 2%

295
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:51,990
of the profit must help

296
00:08:52,030 --> 00:08:53,930
people in the state get treatment.

297
00:08:54,220 --> 00:08:56,230
Supporters call that access.

298
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:58,310
Critics call it a risk.

299
00:08:58,870 --> 00:09:00,830
Most rules for a world like this

300
00:09:00,870 --> 00:09:02,360
have never been written.

301
00:09:02,790 --> 00:09:03,920
How many children,

302
00:09:03,960 --> 00:09:05,660
if so few of us go?

303
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:07,500
One projection for Sweden

304
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:08,960
suggests its population

305
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:10,280
would grow more slowly

306
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:11,410
than many fear.

307
00:09:11,710 --> 00:09:14,060
Even so, in this year's survey,

308
00:09:14,100 --> 00:09:15,430
1 in 5 would accept

309
00:09:15,470 --> 00:09:16,350
stricter limits

310
00:09:16,390 --> 00:09:17,540
on having children,

311
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,010
for a much longer life.

312
00:09:19,480 --> 00:09:20,480
Would you?

313
00:09:21,390 --> 00:09:23,220
So is yours the first

314
00:09:23,260 --> 00:09:24,710
generation that won't die?

315
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:27,240
The honest answer is a range.

316
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:28,250
The estimates run

317
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:29,450
from the next decade

318
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:31,450
to four centuries from now.

319
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:32,810
And neither the repair

320
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:34,170
nor the copy has worked

321
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:36,070
in a single human being yet.

322
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:38,500
But something has already changed.

323
00:09:38,710 --> 00:09:40,110
With AI, death has

324
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:41,010
moved from the things

325
00:09:41,050 --> 00:09:42,520
we accept to the things

326
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:43,560
we work on.

327
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:44,950
And what we work on,

328
00:09:44,990 --> 00:09:46,160
we have to think through,

329
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,370
before it works.

330
00:09:47,610 --> 00:09:48,660
Do we want this?

331
00:09:48,880 --> 00:09:50,380
What does it do to a life?

332
00:09:50,630 --> 00:09:51,630
Who gets it?

333
00:09:51,830 --> 00:09:53,350
If you could stop dying,

334
00:09:53,490 --> 00:09:54,490
would you?

335
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:56,390
Every generation before us

336
00:09:56,430 --> 00:09:57,870
was given the answer.

337
00:09:58,250 --> 00:09:59,500
We are the first

338
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:01,120
to be asked the question.

339
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:04,120
That's what this channel is for:

340
00:10:04,210 --> 00:10:05,910
making sure superintelligence

341
00:10:05,950 --> 00:10:07,620
goes well for everybody,

342
00:10:07,660 --> 00:10:09,640
and that the answer stays yours.

343
00:10:09,830 --> 00:10:10,830
Want that too?

344
00:10:10,890 --> 00:10:11,890
Then subscribe.

